    "html": "<h1>Quantum Approaches for Decision Making</h1>\n<p><strong>Nathan Yang · MSBAi · Gies College of Business · Eight-week teaching materials</strong></p>\n<p>The course examines how questions shape judgments, how models explain those patterns, and how reasons and control affect the interpretation of a choice. No physics or philosophy background is assumed.</p>\n<p>Watch the recorded videos before each live meeting. Live sessions use cases and workshops to advance assessments. The <a href=\"#schedule\">eight-week teaching schedule</a> moves the proposal to Week 3, freezes collection in Week 4, and places collection in Weeks 5–6, studio work in Week 7, and final presentations and defenses in Week 8.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Weeks</th>\n<th>Materials</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1–4</td>\n<td><a href=\"#book\">Module notes</a> and the original recorded foundations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><a href=\"#agency\">Free Will and Agency</a> and the <a href=\"https://github.com/nene88991999/MSBAi_QuantumCognition/blob/main/04_Module_Notes/M1M5_Philosophy_Primer.docx\">philosophy primer</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><a href=\"#time\">Agency over Time</a> and <a href=\"#agency-practice\">Python practice</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><a href=\"#critique\">Analysis and Peer Critique</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><a href=\"#defense\">Presentations and Individual Defense</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Course Resources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#sessions\">Sixteen live-session decks and guides</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/nene88991999/MSBAi_QuantumCognition/blob/main/07_Lecture_Transcripts/README.md\">Lecture transcripts</a>, including the updated broad introduction and course conclusion.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#github\">GitHub project workflow</a> and the <a href=\"https://github.com/Business-Analytics-at-Gies/msbai-github-for-students\">program student guide</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#assessments\">Assessment briefs</a>, <a href=\"#lumen\">Lumen collection handout</a>, and <a href=\"#schedule\">teaching schedule</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#readings\">Reading guide</a>, <a href=\"#python\">Python notebooks</a>, and <a href=\"#start\">interactive book companion</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"#syllabus\">syllabus</a> and <a href=\"#outline\">course outline</a> cover all eight modules. The course retains 2 credits. The assessment weights total 100%: discussions 30%, team proposal 25%, team report/deck 30%, individual defense 5%, and reflection 10%. Practice is ungraded. Team work contributes 55% and individual work 45%. Canvas supplies exact dates and submission links.</p>\n<p>This is the current student-materials working package. Its established Box location remains <strong>Student Materials/Modules 1-4</strong>, now containing the extension, and its Dropbox counterpart is <strong>MSBAi Quantum Cognition/Student_Materials</strong>. The package ZIP is a matching downloadable copy. The repository contains student-facing files; instructor grading guides stay in the private development folders.</p>\n<p>The examples distinguish constructed values, model-panel results, and evidence about people. Store graded work, credentials, and respondent records only in the approved private locations.</p>\n",
    "search": "  Quantum Approaches for Decision Making\n\n  Nathan Yang · MSBAi · Gies College of Business · Eight-week teaching materials  \n\nThe course examines how questions shape judgments, how models explain those patterns, and how reasons and control affect the interpretation of a choice. No physics or philosophy background is assumed.\n\nWatch the recorded videos before each live meeting. Live sessions use cases and workshops to advance assessments. The [eight-week teaching schedule]( schedule) moves the proposal to Week 3, freezes collection in Week 4, and places collection in Weeks 5–6, studio work in Week 7, and final presentations and defenses in Week 8.\n\n  Weeks   Materials  \n  ---   ---  \n  1–4   [Module notes]( book) and the original recorded foundations  \n  5   [Free Will and Agency]( agency) and the [philosophy primer](https://github.com/nene88991999/MSBAi_QuantumCognition/blob/main/04_Module_Notes/M1M5_Philosophy_Primer.docx)  \n  6   [Agency over Time]( time) and [Python practice]( agency-practice)  \n  7   [Analysis and Peer Critique]( critique)  \n  8   [Presentations and Individual Defense]( defense)  \n\n   Course Resources\n\n- [Sixteen live-session decks and guides]( sessions).\n- [Lecture transcripts](https://github.com/nene88991999/MSBAi_QuantumCognition/blob/main/07_Lecture_Transcripts/README.md), including the updated broad introduction and course conclusion.\n- [GitHub project workflow]( github) and the [program student guide](https://github.com/Business-Analytics-at-Gies/msbai-github-for-students).\n- [Assessment briefs]( assessments), [Lumen collection handout]( lumen), and [teaching schedule]( schedule).\n- [Reading guide]( readings), [Python notebooks]( python), and [interactive book companion]( start).\n\nThe [syllabus]( syllabus) and [course outline]( outline) cover all eight modules. The course retains 2 credits. The assessment weights total 100%: discussions 30%, team proposal 25%, team report/deck 30%, individual defense 5%, and reflection 10%. Practice is ungraded. Team work contributes 55% and individual work 45%. Canvas supplies exact dates and submission links.\n\nThis is the current student-materials working package. Its established Box location remains   Student Materials/Modules 1-4  , now containing the extension, and its Dropbox counterpart is   MSBAi Quantum Cognition/Student_Materials  . The package ZIP is a matching downloadable copy. The repository contains student-facing files; instructor grading guides stay in the private development folders.\n\nThe examples distinguish constructed values, model-panel results, and evidence about people. Store graded work, credentials, and respondent records only in the approved private locations.\n"
